We can copy the patterns of Armani, Christian Dior, whatever youd like, says Abdul, who has been plying his trade for three decades now. Most people are asymmetrical but designer suits off-the-rack are sewn symmetrically and, even with alterations, dont fit as well as one would if it had been made to fit your body right from the start.
Even if you factor in the price of a flight to Singapore, which will run you as much as $3,000, you can still get three suits for the same price you'd spend on a name-brand. But how can something that claims to be high-quality cost so little? Abdul says its simple: low overhead. He then took Esquire.com on a tour of his factories for a closer look of the process.
Tailor shops throughout Singapore consist of shelves stacked high with hundreds of bolts of fabrics. (And if you dont see what youre looking for, there are dozens of sample books to flip through.) We buy our fabrics direct from Italy, says Abdul, so we have the best silks, cashmeres, and Italian wools at a lower price. This stock includes a range of lightweight super 100s to 200s. But even still, a suit made of their finest, most-expensive fabric, Vitale Barberis Canonico, will only cost about $1,200.
