Taking pictures in nature has been a favorite occupation of photographers since the early days of the camera.
In the mid-1800s, scenery was the primary subject; film was slow, exposures were long and scenery didn`t move. In America, photographers discovered the wonders of the Rocky Mountains, the deserts, the falls and such spectacles as the Grand Tetons and Yellowstone Park.
People could be in the pictures if they stood still, a possibility that a German photographer, Theodor Wundt, exploited in 1893 when he climbed the Alps and took along a model, Jeanne Immink, to pose at the edge of abysses.
By 1890 high-speed shutters had enabled photographers to stop locomotion, animals had become photo subjects and the wealthier photographers took their cameras with them when they went on hunting safaris to Africa.
In 1924, English author Marius Maxwell warned readers of the dangers of photographing wild elephants: ''Face-to-face meetings are at all times nasty incidents, and particularly speculative at close quarters.'' There were, he wrote, 11 sounds that gave clues to an elephant`s mood. (A low squeak means contentment and a rumbling noise indicates alarm, in case you need to know someday.)
Maxwell`s advice on what to do when faced with a charging rhino probably would not be heeded by even the coolest of photographers: ''A sidestep in the nick of time is possible, but requires agility, and remains at all times a risky performance.''
Fortunately, the photographer of today need not be placed in such a position. Long telephoto lenses enable the photographer to get close-ups of animals without any of the risks of being close, and in conservation-conscious Africa, safaris now cater almost exclusively to those with cameras. They are still expensive, however.
A photographer with a heavy interest in animals but a light wallet need not go to Africa to photograph wild animals. Most of them are in the local zoo.
Years ago, someone suggested that in the Bronx Zoo, for the investment of a day and a nickel, you could see animals that it would take you 10 years and thousands of dollars to see in the wild.
