Buster Posey supposedly hates it when people refer to the catcher-interference rule as the "Posey Rule," but because it was instituted in 2014 as a result of his 2011 injury from a home-plate collision, he'll just have to suck it up.
You don't see many home-plate collisions anymore because of the Posey Rule, which is good for catchers but bad for those who prefer the game to be played the way it was meant to be played.
But Monday night at Wrigley Field we saw a good old-fashioned collision between Anthony Rizzo and Padres catcher Austin Hedges during the sixth inning of the Cubs' 3-2 victory.
With one out and Rizzo on third, Padres center fielder Matt Szczur caught a Kris Bryant line drive and fired a nice throw to the plate. Rizzo barreled into Hedges, who held onto the ball to complete the double play.
It was a bang-bang play that brought the crowd to its feet and had everyone buzzing, a moment that was replayed over and over on sports-highlight shows.
Hedges suffered a bruised right thigh from the collision and had to be taken out of the game. The play drew the ire of Padres manager Andy Green, who later called it a "cheap shot" and hinted Rizzo deserved a suspension.
